# Rank 1691 / CVE-2026-26267 (GHSA-4chv-4c6w-w254, recorded CWE-670; the
# measured shape is wrong-function resolution at an exported entry point):
# soroban-sdk's #[contractimpl] proc macro emitted the WASM export wrapper for
# a trait impl block as `<Type>::func()`, and Rust's name resolution prefers
# an inherent associated function over a trait function for that syntax, so a
# contract whose type carried an inherent method sharing the trait method's
# name exported the INHERENT implementation: the on-chain entry point ran
# different code than the #[contractimpl] block annotated. e92a3933 ("Fix
# trait method call resolution in contractimpl (#1729)", 2026-02-14) emits
# `<Type as Trait>::func()` when a trait is in play. The vulnerable construct
# is present at the parent (soroban-sdk-macros/src/derive_fn.rs:52, `let call
# = quote! { <#impl_ty>::#ident };`) and the remediation at the commit.
#
# ATTENTION, no layer can see the defect, three ways measured.
#
# 1. Where the change lands (the proc macro), the only discriminator between the
#    revisions is the fixing commit's own layout. The vulnerable token text
#    `quote! { <#impl_ty>::#ident }` is still present at the commit -- it is
#    the else branch of the same `let` -- so any token- or text-keyed binding
#    fires identically on both revisions; only the presence of `as #t` (or the
#    `if let Some(t) = trait_ident` wrapper) distinguishes, which keys on the
#    fix's own spelling of a private metavariable. Measured on both revisions
#    of the real tree: 1390 findings / 135 high on each, and the only two
#    findings in soroban-sdk-macros are VYQL-MEM-006 NullableDeref noise at
#    derive_fn.rs:58 and :116, unchanged at both revisions.
#
# 2. Where the defect manifests (consumer contracts), the trigger is an
#    inherent method colliding with a #[contractimpl] trait-impl method on the
#    same type. An exhaustive sweep of every impl in the tree at the parent
#    (270 Rust files; 5 types carry #[contractimpl] trait impls) finds zero
#    true collisions -- the single name-keyed cross-file hit joins three
#    DISTINCT per-module `Contract` types (contract_duration.rs,
#    contract_timepoint.rs, contract_custom_account_impl.rs), which is the
#    name-join trap, not a collision. The only instance ever written is the
#    regression test the change itself adds, so a consumer-side binding reports
#    MORE on the fixed revision: inverted discrimination, and an empty
#    reachable set outside this incident.
#
# 3. The facts a consumer-side binding would need do not exist. #[contractimpl]
#    sits on the impl block, and the Rust frontend drops impl-item attributes
#    at the impl boundary: stmtH's "impl_item" case
#    (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/rust.go) ignores the attrs its
#    caller collected and re-walks the body with a fresh decls(), and no fact
#    carries the impl's trait or self type. Minimal pair, measured: the same
#    attribute on a function yields an analysis.parameter.entry fact a
#    binding can key on (args.any.context.attrName -- a probe binding emitted
#    code.HttpInput from it), while the identical attribute on an impl block
#    yields zero facts (vyql query -call analysis.parameter.entry: 1 node vs
#    0). That is the Rust no-impl-level-fact family recorded at rank
#    1364. Even with impl attributes and impl identity, the collision itself
#    is a cross-item same-name join between two sibling impl blocks that only
#    real type identity can validate -- the sweep's false hit above is what a
#    name-only join produces -- and no binding construct expresses it
#    (scopeCall is call-shaped and same-or-nested lexical scopes).
#
# The blocks below pin the measured zeros on the real shapes. The rejects name
# VYQL-MEM-006, the only rule family that reports anywhere in this repository
# (992 of 1390 findings), to assert the scanner reads these files and judges
# nothing about the defect. The first block is the fixed revision's own regression
# contract (the trigger shape); when an impl-identity fact and a
# collision/export rule exist, it must flip to expect. The second block is the
# repository's real no-collision trait impl (CustomAccountInterface). The
# third is the safe inherent-impl form that any naive contractimpl-keyed
# binding would have to stay silent on -- telling it apart from the trait form
# needs exactly the impl trait-identity fact that is missing.
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1691: a contractimpl trait method sharing a name with an inherent method"
  lang rust
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  use soroban_sdk::{contract, contractimpl, Env};

  #[contract]
  pub struct Contract;

  impl Contract {
      pub fn value(_env: Env) -> u32 {
          1
      }
  }

  pub trait ContractTrait {
      fn value(env: Env) -> u32;
  }

  #[contractimpl]
  impl ContractTrait for Contract {
      fn value(_env: Env) -> u32 {
          2
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1691: a contractimpl trait method with no same-named inherent method"
  lang rust
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  use soroban_sdk::{
      auth::{Context, CustomAccountInterface},
      contract, contracterror, contractimpl,
      crypto::Hash,
      Env, Vec,
  };

  #[contract]
  pub struct Contract;

  #[contracterror]
  #[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord)]
  pub enum Error {
      Fail = 1,
  }

  #[contractimpl]
  impl CustomAccountInterface for Contract {
      type Signature = ();
      type Error = Error;

      fn __check_auth(
          _env: Env,
          _signature_payload: Hash<32>,
          _signatures: Self::Signature,
          _auth_contexts: Vec<Context>,
      ) -> Result<(), Self::Error> {
          Ok(())
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1691: a contractimpl inherent impl, where unqualified resolution is correct"
  lang rust
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  use soroban_sdk::{contract, contractimpl, Duration};

  #[contract]
  pub struct Contract;

  #[contractimpl]
  impl Contract {
      pub fn exec(_d: Duration) {}
  }
  ```
